config CTCC_APP_PROTECT
	bool "CTHINGS.CO APPROTECT lock helper"
	depends on !ARM_NONSECURE_FIRMWARE
	default y
	help
	  Compile the app_protect SYS_INIT helper. When CONFIG_NRF_APPROTECT_LOCK is
	  selected it writes UICR.APPROTECT and resets, making the debug lock
	  PERMANENT: it survives a power cycle and re-flashing over SWD then needs
	  `nrfjprog --recover`.

	  SECURE images only. UICR is reachable only from the secure side, so on a
	  non-secure image the helper could do nothing but add a dead SYS_INIT entry -
	  hence `depends on !ARM_NONSECURE_FIRMWARE`. On these cards that means the
	  bootloader does the locking, before any application is installed.

	  What each family needs from it differs, and the helper covers both:

	  nRF52840 has ONE access port, and its UICR word is the only thing that makes a
	  lock PERMANENT: nothing in Zephyr or NCS writes that word. What NCS does do,
	  with NRF_APPROTECT_HANDLING at LOCK, is compile the MDK's SystemInit with
	  ENABLE_APPROTECT, which force-protects the port on every boot. So without this
	  helper the port is still closed while our firmware runs - it is not "open" -
	  but the protection is volatile and an erase plus reflash removes it. The helper
	  is what survives that.

	  nRF91 has TWO, and NCS does handle both: with the NRF_APPROTECT_HANDLING and
	  NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_HANDLING choices at LOCK (defaulted at board level),
	  SystemInit force-protects each on every boot. But FORCEPROTECT is volatile -
	  it protects a card while our firmware runs and is gone the moment the part is
	  erased and reflashed. This helper writes both UICR words as well, which is
	  what makes the lock survive that. The two mechanisms are complementary: NCS
	  closes the ports early in every boot, the UICR write makes the state
	  permanent from reset.

	  This applies to a SECURE image, which is the only kind the helper is built
	  into. In a non-secure (/ns) application TF-M holds the secure side and writes
	  both UICR words itself, permanently, via nrfx_nvmc_word_write() - so such a
	  card is already locked for good and the helper is neither present nor needed.

	  Each write is gated on its own choice, so an image that selects
	  NRF_APPROTECT_LOCK without NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_LOCK locks only the port it
	  asked for, and the secure-port write compiles out entirely on nRF52840.

	  Disabling this alone does NOT keep a card debuggable. It removes the permanent
	  UICR write, but the per-boot force-protect comes from the NRF_APPROTECT_HANDLING
	  choice, not from here - so a debuggable image also has to select
	  NRF_APPROTECT_USE_UICR (and, on nRF91, NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_USE_UICR for the
	  second port). Set all of them for a build that must stay debuggable: a bring-up
	  image, a sample, or a hardware test that must not lock the card it qualifies.
	  The assembly test in this repository is the worked example.
